{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "וישמהו על הנס", :translit "va'yasimehu al ha-nes", :gloss "and he set it upon the standard"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Analogy to a father placing the whip high so the son sees it and remembers."}], :frame {:text "The verse describes Moses setting the copper serpent upon a standard (nes). The Ba'al HaTurim reads nes in its sense of a raised banner or pole, drawing a pedagogical analogy: just as a father who disciplines a son places the instrument of rebuke in a visible spot so the child remembers, so too the serpent was elevated for all to see and remember. This resonates with the verse's own statement that one who was bitten would look upon the serpent and live (Numbers 21:9), and with Proverbs 3:12, 'for whom the LORD loves He rebukes, even as a father the son in whom he delights.'", :sources ["Numbers 21:9" "Proverbs 3:12"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Analogy to a father placing the whip high so the son sees it and remembers.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:30:22.615314359Z", :model "glm-5.2"}